# Deterministic scalar extraction, shared verbatim modulo fact tags and step numbering.
def diagonal_parts(prefix,bw,prior,T):
 p1=rf'{prefix}.1 Define a canonical extraction $E$ for a bounded real sequence $(b_n)_{{n\ge1}}$. Let $B\ge1$ be the least integer with $|b_n|\le B$ for every $n$, and put $I_0=[-B,B]$. Having defined $I_\ell$, bisect it into its two closed halves and define $I_{{\ell+1}}$ to be the left half if that half contains $b_n$ for infinitely many indices $n$, and the right half otherwise. At least one half contains infinitely many such indices, so this rule is defined and unique. Set $e_0=0$ and let $e_\ell$ be the least $n>e_{{\ell-1}}$ with $b_n\in I_\ell$. These minima exist because the corresponding index sets are infinite. Then $E(b)(\ell)=e_\ell$ is strictly increasing and $(b_{{e_\ell}})$ is Cauchy: for $p,q\ge\ell$, both values lie in $I_\ell$, whose length is $2B2^{{-\ell}}$. It converges, since [{bw}] gives a convergent subsequence of this bounded Cauchy sequence and the Cauchy inequality forces the entire sequence to have the same limit. All intervals and indices are uniquely specified; this defines a function $E$ without any choice principle. [given, {bw}, construct, algebra]'
 p2=rf'{prefix}.2 Enumerate the real and imaginary coefficient coordinates, Fourier modes, and rational times in $[0,{T}]$ by a fixed explicit integer-height and lexicographic enumeration; denote the resulting bounded real sequences in the approximation index $N$ by $b^1,b^2,\ldots$. Starting with $h_0(n)=n$, define recursively $h_i=h_{{i-1}}\circ E(b^i\circ h_{{i-1}})$ and put $N_\ell=h_\ell(\ell)$. The successor in this recursion is a specified function of $h_{{i-1}}$, so this is ordinary recursion, not a succession of unspecified dependent selections. Each $h_i$ is strictly increasing and its range is contained in every preceding range. Moreover $N_{{\ell+1}}=h_\ell(E(b^{{\ell+1}}\circ h_\ell)(\ell+1))\ge h_\ell(\ell+1)>N_\ell$. For each fixed $i$, the tail $(N_\ell)_{{\ell\ge i}}$ is an increasing subsequence of the range of $h_i$, on which $b^i$ converges. Thus this one subsequence converges at every enumerated coefficient-time coordinate. Countable Choice remains the standing analytic Fourier assumption; the diagonal construction itself does not require dependent choice. [step {prior}, step {prefix}.1, construct, algebra]'
 p3=rf'{prefix}.3 The equicontinuity from step {prior} upgrades convergence at rational times to uniform-in-time convergence of each fixed Fourier coefficient. Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose a finite rational time grid so that every time is close enough to a grid point to make each of the two equicontinuity errors below $\varepsilon/3$; choose the subsequence indices large enough that the finitely many grid values differ by less than $\varepsilon/3$. The triangle inequality makes each coordinate uniformly Cauchy, and scalar completeness supplies its uniform limit. [step {prior}, step {prefix}.2, {bw}, algebra]'
 return '\n\n'.join([p1,p2,p3])
